    "question": {
      ":question/id": "070438a9-24df-57a5-838b-ab1f480b47ed",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\sqrt{x}\\left(-x+3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x$ for $x>0$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt{x}\\left(-x+3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x &= \\int \\left(- x^{\\frac{3}{2}} + 3 \\sqrt{x}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=-\\int x^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x+3\\int x^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=-\\left(\\frac{x^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{\\frac{5}{2}}\\right)+3\\left(\\frac{x^{\\frac{3}{2}}}{\\frac{3}{2}}\\right)+C \\\\ &=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{x^{5}}}{5}+2\\sqrt{x^{3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
